{"summary":"Full source ledger traced. The disclosure (identical text in the single USER turn of conversation.md) affirmatively supports essentially every substantive limitation in the draft: two semicircular frame halves with hinge at one end and releasable latch at the other; three radial jaw carriages each sliding in a radial guide and carrying a pivoting concave jaw pad that needs no pipe-size-specific part; a segmented cam ring retained in an annular guide with one segment per frame half; complementary drive/coupling faces at the hinge and latch ends that couple on closing so the segments move as one ring; a follower per carriage in a respective oblique slot; a wedge driven by the latch against a drive face of the ring, rotating the coupled ring through a limited stroke; synchronized inward travel of all three carriages; a spring biasing each carriage outward on release; the latch's holding position and over-center clamping position; the force-limiting spring stack between handle and wedge absorbing residual handle travel; the indicator pin moving relative to a fixed edge of the latch housing at a predetermined compression range and reporting load rather than diameter; the install/remove sequence; and the optional mounting boss outside the annular guide for a sensor, tool, or conduit that does not participate in jaw synchronization. Claims 1-15, the Summary, the Detailed Description, the Abstract, and all 25 reference numerals trace to that text. No drafting notes, placeholders, open questions, or unresolved alternatives remain, and every numeral in numerals.md is used consistently and appears on at least one figure.

I found no critical or major fidelity gaps. Four minor items remain: one figure brief (FIG. 4) makes three unhedged geometric/structural choices that the disclosure leaves open (an interruption in the radially outer frame wall, the radially inward direction of wedge advance, and a hole in the housing top wall for the indicator pin) and should be added to that brief's existing "shown schematically" list rather than to the application text; the FIG. 1 brief uses the bare word "ring" for the annular frame body, which the application text reserves for the cam ring/coupled ring; the Detailed Description calls coupling faces 26/28 "drive faces", blurring them against "ring drive face 36"; and claims 8 and 10 recite "the synchronized advance of the jaw carriages" without a literal antecedent in claim 1's "advances the three jaw carriages inwardly in synchronism".

Two contextual notes, not findings. First, I verified the drawing geometry is mutually consistent: FIG. 4 is viewed from the side opposite FIG. 1, so the wedge driving the band leftward (away from first frame half 10) corresponds to the clockwise sense stated in FIG. 2, and FIG. 2's slot orientation (inner end counterclockwise of outer end) does produce inward carriage travel under that sense. Second, prior_art/INDEX.md lists no documents, so the Background's characterizations of existing split clamps, band/two-bolt saddle arrangements, liners machined to one nominal size, and torque tools carry no citations and have nothing in prior_art/ to trace to; per this preflight's scope that tracing belongs to the later citation review, but the drafter should be aware there is no art on file to support it.","findings":[{"severity":"minor","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"FIG. 4 brief makes three unhedged structural/kinematic choices the disclosure leaves open","where":"figures/FIG-4.md, opening note and body (lines 3, 5, 9, 15)","detail":"The FIG. 4 brief lists what is shown schematically ('the latch housing and its cavity, the gap through which the latch handle 50 passes, the linkage by which it acts on the force-limiting spring stack 54, and the coupling of the indicator pin 56 to that stack') but then asserts three further features that are renderer choices, not disclosed structure: (a) an interruption in the radially outer frame wall at the latch end; (b) the wedge advancing radially inward ('downward movement drives the band to the left'); and (c) a hole in the housing top wall through which the indicator pin passes. The disclosure says only 'Closing the latch drives a wedge against a drive face of the cam ring' and 'A visible indicator pin moves relative to a fixed edge of the latch housing'; it never specifies a window in the guide wall, the direction of wedge advance, or a through-hole. The Detailed Description is deliberately neutral on the wedge direction ('it lies at an inclination to the direction in which that wedge advances'), so these are depiction conventions that need to be labelled as such and kept confined to the figure brief. No application text should be added to justify them.","evidence":"FIG-4.md line 5: 'the frame wall radially outside the band being interrupted at the latch end so that the upper side of the band is exposed and the wedge 52 can reach the ring drive face 36'; line 9: 'Its lower left face slants at the same angle as the ring drive face 36 and bears on it, so downward movement drives the band to the left'; line 15: 'A slender pin, the indicator pin 56, rises from the force-limiting spring stack 54 through a hole in the top wall.' Nearest source: disclosure.md line 7, 'Closing the latch drives a wedge against a drive face of the cam ring and rotates the coupled ring through a limited stroke', and line 9, 'A visible indicator pin moves relative to a fixed edge of the latch housing when the spring stack enters a predetermined compression range.' Neither passage specifies any of the three features.","fix":"In figures/FIG-4.md line 3, extend the existing schematic list to read: 'Shown schematically: the latch housing and its cavity, the interruption of the frame wall radially outside the band at the latch end, the direction in which the wedge 52 advances, the gap through which the latch handle 50 passes, the linkage by which it acts on the force-limiting spring stack 54, the opening in the top wall through which the indicator pin 56 passes, and the coupling of the indicator pin 56 to that stack.' Make no change to draft/07-drawings.md or draft/08-detailed-description.md."},{"severity":"minor","category":"terminology","title":"FIG. 1 brief uses bare "ring" for the annular frame body, a term the application reserves for the cam ring","where":"figures/FIG-1.md lines 7, 9, 13, 15, 17, 23","detail":"Throughout the application text, 'ring' denotes the segmented cam ring 20 / the coupled ring, while the closed frame body is called 'a frame that is annular about the axis' or 'the frame'. The FIG. 1 brief instead calls the frame annulus 'a ring of substantial width', and then refers to 'the outer boundary of the ring', 'the inner boundary of the ring', and 'spaced around the ring'. Because numeral 20 (segmented cam ring) is not shown on FIG. 1, no wrong label results directly, but the brief instructs a renderer to place numeral 100 on 'the outer boundary of the ring', which is ambiguous against the application's fixed meaning of 'ring' and risks a mislabelled part.","evidence":"FIG-1.md line 7: 'A ring of substantial width surrounds the pipe 90, bounded by an inner circle spaced outward from it and an outer circle.' FIG-1.md line 23: '100 split pipe clamp: target the outer boundary of the ring at its upper right.' Compare draft/08-detailed-description.md line 5: 'the first frame half 10 and the second frame half 12 define a frame that is annular about the axis of the clamp 100', and line 13 et seq., where 'ring' always means the segmented cam ring 20.","fix":"In figures/FIG-1.md, replace every bare 'ring' referring to the frame body with 'annular frame body': line 7 to 'An annular frame body of substantial width surrounds the pipe 90...'; line 9 'divide the annular frame body into two semicircular bodies'; line 13 'lying outside the outer boundary of the annular frame body and bridging both frame ends, with an elongated lever extending outward from the block, away from the annular frame body'; line 15 'Three jaw carriages 30 are spaced around the annular frame body... Each crosses the inner boundary of the annular frame body'; line 19 'extending past the outer boundary of the annular frame body at one end'; line 23 '100 split pipe clamp: target the outer boundary of the annular frame body at its upper right.'"},{"severity":"minor","category":"terminology","title":"Detailed Description calls coupling faces 26/28 "drive faces", blurring them against ring drive face 36","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, paragraph beginning "The first cam ring segment 22 and the second cam ring segment 24 meet at two places" (line 15)","detail":"The application otherwise maintains a clean two-term split, matching the claims: 'complementary coupling faces' 26/28 (segment-to-segment) and 'ring drive face' 36 (wedge-to-ring). One sentence in the Detailed Description calls 26 and 28 'circumferentially facing drive faces', which makes 'drive face' mean two different interfaces in the same document and could be read as giving 26/28 the same role as 36. The claims (claims 1, 7, 10, 13) never use 'drive face' for 26/28, so the description alone is out of step. The disclosure's own wording is 'complementary drive faces', but the draft has already elected 'coupling faces' throughout the claims, so the description should follow that election consistently.","evidence":"draft/08-detailed-description.md line 15: 'The complementary coupling faces 26 and the complementary coupling faces 28 are circumferentially facing drive faces, each pair being shaped so that one segment can transmit circumferential force to the other through the joint.' Compare draft/09-claims.md claim 1: 'having complementary coupling faces at the hinge end and complementary coupling faces at the latch end' and, separately, 'positioned to bear on a ring drive face of the segmented cam ring'.","fix":"In draft/08-detailed-description.md line 15, change 'are circumferentially facing drive faces' to 'are circumferentially facing coupling faces'. Leave the rest of the sentence unchanged."},{"severity":"minor","category":"formalities","title":"Claims 8 and 10 recite "the synchronized advance of the jaw carriages" without antecedent basis","where":"draft/09-claims.md, claim 8 and claim 10 (mounting-boss element)","detail":"Claim 1 recites the function as 'rotation of the coupled ring through a stroke advances the three jaw carriages inwardly in synchronism'. Neither claim 1 nor the body of claim 10 ever recites 'a synchronized advance', so the definite phrase 'the synchronized advance of the jaw carriages' in claim 8 and in claim 10's mounting-boss element lacks a literal antecedent under 35 U.S.C. 112(b). The underlying limitation is fully supported by the disclosure ('The mounting boss does not participate in jaw synchronization'); only the claim wording needs aligning.","evidence":"draft/09-claims.md claim 8: 'the mounting boss not participating in the synchronized advance of the jaw carriages'; claim 10: 'a mounting boss on the frame outside the annular guide, the mounting boss not participating in the synchronized advance of the jaw carriages'. Claim 1's only recitation is: 'each oblique slot being inclined to a radial direction of the frame such that rotation of the coupled ring through a stroke advances the three jaw carriages inwardly in synchronism'. Source: disclosure.md line 13, 'The mounting boss does not participate in jaw synchronization.'","fix":"In draft/09-claims.md, in claim 8 and in the mounting-boss element of claim 10, replace 'the synchronized advance of the jaw carriages' with 'the advance of the three jaw carriages inwardly in synchronism'."}]}
